After mining the old \(KN\) scattering data, no resonance structure has been seen and the \(\Theta^{+}\) width has been constrained to be less than a few MeV [23, 24, 25]. Detailed summary of the experimental situation on pentaquark searches can be found in the following references: [17, 26, 27]. Overall, the positive evidence for the existence of pentaquark particles is diminishing and the remaining positive results need to be tested.

In 1997, Diakonov et al. proposed the possible existence of a \(S=1\), \(J^{P}=\frac{1}{2}^{+}\) resonance (the \(\Theta^{+}\)) at 1530 MeV with a width less than 15 MeV by using the chiral soliton model [10], which partly motivated the recent experimental

Figure 1.3: The \(\overline{10}\) pentaquarks predicted by Chiral Soliton Model. \(x\) axis is the third component of isospin (\(I_{3}\)) while \(y\) axis is the hypercharge \(Y=B+S\).

search of this particle. The Chiral Soliton Model is based on the Skyrme soliton (Skyrmion) model of the baryon, which utilizes a topological soliton solution in the \(SU(3)\times SU(3)\) chiral symmetric nonlinear sigma model. Their model has specific \(SU(3)\) breaking terms, indicated by the chiral quark model, but the strengths of the individual terms of the effective Lagrangian are determined phenomenologically [10]. According to this model calculation, the \(\Theta^{+}\) is the lightest member of the anti-decuplet multiplet (\(\overline{10}\)) in the third rotational state. The anti-decuplet members are shown in figure 1.3. In this figure, the three members lie in the corner have explicit exotic quantum numbers and thus are explicit exotic particles, i.e. they can not be explained by conventional two or three quark states.

After the Chiral Soliton Model prediction by Diakonov et al. and the subsequent reported observation by various experiments, there have appeared numerous theoretical models trying to explain the low mass and narrow width of the observed \(\Theta^{+}\). These model calculations includes the diquark model [11], clustered quark model [12], constituent quark model [13], QCD sum rules [14] etc. These theoretical calculations are nicely summarized in a few papers [15, 16, 17].

In the diquark model, \(\Theta^{+}\) is composed of two diquarks and a strange anti-quark, where the diquark is a strongly correlated quark pair. In all quark models, including conventional quark model, clustered quark models and diquark model, a small decay width for the \(\Theta^{+}\) is hard to be accommodated, due to the fall-apart decay mode of five quark system. In this decay mode, no extra quark antiquark pair needs to be created for pentaquark to decay into a meson and a baryon since there are already four quarks and an antiquark around.

People are also trying to look for pentaquark structures in Monte-Carlo lattice QCD simulations (see [10] and references therein). Similar to the experimental situation, several LQCD calculations found positive evidence for the pentaquark while others reported null results. Among those positive results, there is disagreements regarding the spin and parity of the ground state.

S. Capstick et al. [3] have raised the possibility for the \(\Theta^{+}\) to be an isotensor (\(I=2\)) state. There will be an isospin multiplet containing \(\Theta^{-}(dddd\bar{s})\), \(\Theta^{0}(uddd\bar{s})\), \(\Theta^{+}(uudd\bar{s})\), \(\Theta^{++}(uuud\bar{s})\) and \(\Theta^{+++}(uuuu\bar{s})\) if this hypothesis is true. Of course, the \(\Theta^{+}\) isospin can also possibly be one thus the multiplet contains only three members \(\Theta^{0}(uddd\bar{s})\), \(\Theta^{+}(uudd\bar{s})\) and \(\Theta^{++}(uuud\bar{s})\). Besides the \(\Theta^{+}\), the proposed decay channel \(\Theta^{++}\to p+K^{+}\) is very well suited for experimental investigations since the decayed daughters are easily to be detected and identified. However, several experiments [11, 12, 13, 14, 15] reported null results on searches of \(\Theta^{++}\) through decay channel \(\Theta^{++}\to p+K^{+}\) and concluded that the \(\Theta^{+}\) state should be isosinglet.

In relativistic heavy ion collisions, it has been found that the production of hadron in the intermediate \(p_{t}\) region can be explained nicely by quark recombination/coalescence models (see [1]). If this scenario is true and the pentaquark particles really exist, then heavy ion collisions at RHIC may be a good environment that favors the production of pentaquark particles by just combining the available copious constituent quarks together. It is therefore very important that at RHIC we perform the search for pentaquark particles to help clarify the experimental situation.

Based on its design, STAR is well suited for the search of pentaquark particles. The large acceptance and the tracking capability of the TPC have made STAR able to identify various short-living particles such as the \(\phi\), \(K^{*}\), \(\Lambda^{*}\), \(K^{0}_{S}\), \(\Lambda\), \(\Xi^{-}\) and \(\Omega\) by utilizing either event mixing techniques or reconstruction of displaceddecay vertices. Stable particles such as \(\pi^{\pm}\), \(K^{\pm}\) and \(p(\bar{p})\) can be identified as well by measuring their ionization energy loss in the TPC gas.

Here we present the results for the search of \(\Theta^{++}\to p+K^{+}\), its anti-particle \(\bar{\Theta}^{--}\rightarrow\bar{p}+K^{-}\), and also \(\Xi^{--}\rightarrow\Xi^{-}+\pi^{-}\) and its anti-particle \(\bar{\Xi}^{++}\rightarrow\bar{\Xi}^{+}+\pi^{+}\) from the STAR experiment at RHIC. Charged hadrons \(\pi^{\pm}K^{\pm}p\bar{p}\) are identified by their ionization energy loss in the TPC gas. The \(pK\) invariant mass is calculated to search for the \(\Theta^{++}\to p+K^{+}\) signal. For the \(\Xi^{--}\rightarrow\Xi^{-}+\pi^{-}\) pentaquark, the \(\Xi^{-}\) and \(\Lambda\) are reconstructed by finding their displaced decay vertices from the decay channel \(\Xi^{-}\rightarrow\Lambda+\pi^{-}\) and \(\Lambda\to p+\pi^{-}\) respectively.

From now on, we shall refer to the \(\Theta^{++}\) and \(\Xi^{--}\) as implicitly including their anti-particles unless specified otherwise. It's also worth keeping in mind that the names used for pentaquarks in this thesis are for convenience only, and the names do not imply that we claim the existence of these particles.

### 1.5 Thesis outline

In this thesis we present the measurement of \(\phi\) meson production as well as the results for the searches of pentaquark particles at RHIC from the STAR experiment. In chapter 2 we will briefly introduce the experimental facilities. This is followed in chapter 3 by the description of the analysis methods and by the presentation of results for the \(\phi\) meson measurements. In chapter 4 we will present the analysis details and results for the searches of pentaquark particles from STAR. Finally, in chapter 5 we will conclude and talk about further measurements that are needed for both the \(\phi\) and the pentaquark particles.

## Chapter 2 Experimental Facilities

In this chapter the facilities built for the heavy ion program are briefly described. We will describe the collider complex, the STAR experiment and the main tracking device-the TPC for STAR.

### 2.1 RHIC collider

The Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC) at Brookhaven National Laboratory (BNL) is a world-class scientific research facility that was built to create and investigate strongly interacting matter at energy densities unprecedented in a laboratory setting. It is the first machine that can bring heavy ions to head-on collisions. The purpose of this extraordinary new accelerator is to seek out and explore new high-energy forms of matter and thus continue the centuries-old quest to understand the nature and origins of matter at its most basic level. Hundreds of physicists from around the world use RHIC to study what the universe may have looked like in the first few moments after its creation. RHIC drives two intersecting beams of gold ions head-on, in a subatomic collision. What physicists learn from these collisions may help us understand more about why the physical world works the way it does, from the smallest subatomic particles, to the largest stars [RHI].

Figure 2.1 shows the components of RHIC complex. Some designed parameavy ions and also (polarized) protons. Its two counter-circulating superconducting storage rings are arbitrarily denoted as blue and yellow. The RHIC double storage ring is itself hexagonally shaped and 3834 m long in circumference, with curved edges in which stored particles are deflected by 1,740 superconducting niobium titanium magnets. The six interaction points are at the middle of the six relatively straight sections, where the two rings cross, allowing the particles to collide. The interaction points are enumerated by clock positions, with the injection point at 6 o'clock. Four interaction points are occupied by experimental detector systems, STAR at 6 o'clock, PHENIX at 8 o'clock, PHOBOS at 10 o'clock and BRAHMS at 2 o'clock, respectively. The four experimental programs complement each other with emphasis on different physics goals. Two interaction points are unused and left for further expansion.

