#### Nuclear modification factors \(R_{cp}\) and \(R_{aa}\)

A comparison of the \(R_{CP}\) for the \(\phi\), \(K_{S}^{0}\) and \(\Lambda\) is shown in figure 3.14 (a). Both statistical and systematic errors are included in the figure. The ratio \(R_{AA}\) for central (top 5%) and peripheral (60-80%) \(Au+Au\) data are shown in figure 3.14 (b) and (c), respectively. \(R_{AA}\) for charged hadrons [1] is also shown as a reference. The charged hadron and \(\phi\) peripheral \(R_{AA}\) both go above the binary scaling limit, but are consistent with unity within the systematic uncertainties. The \(\phi\) central \(R_{AA}\) approaches unity and point to point is higher than \(R_{CP}\). With the systematic uncertainty on the normalization of the ratio, however, both \(R_{AA}\) and \(R_{CP}\) are consistent. Note that a \(R_{AA}\) ratio that is higher than the \(R_{CP}\) ratio would be consistent with OZI suppression of \(\phi\) production in \(p+p\)[2, 30, 31] and/or strangeness enhancement in \(Au+Au\) collisions. A measurement of \(R_{AA}\) vs. system size may be sensitive to the system size at which OZI becomes irrelevant to \(\phi\) production.

The \(\phi\)\(R_{CP}\) result is consistent with partonic recombination models [13, 14, 15, 16]. In these models, hadronization proceeds by recombining 2 (3) quarks to form mesons (baryons) up to intermediate \(p_{t}\) region. Thus the centrality dependence of the yield at intermediate \(p_{t}\) depends on the number Figure 3.14: \(R_{CP}\) (a): The ratio of central (top 5%) over peripheral (60-80%) (\(R_{CP}\)) normalized by \(\langle N_{bin}\rangle\). The ratios for the \(\Lambda\) and \(K^{0}_{S}\), shown by dotted-dashed and dashed lines, are taken from [1]; \(R_{AA}\) (b) and (c) are the ratios of central \(Au+Au\) (top 5%) to \(p+p\) and peripheral \(Au+Au\) (60-80%) to \(p+p\), respectively. The values of \(R_{AA}\) for charged hadrons are shown as open circles [1]. The width of the gray bands represent the uncertainties in the estimation of \(\langle N_{bin}\rangle\) summed in quadrature with the normalization uncertainties of the spectra. Errors on the \(\phi\) meson data points are the statistical plus 15% systematic errors.

of constituent quarks rather than on the particle mass. The STAR measurement of \(R_{CP}\) for various hadrons supports the prediction from recombination models (see figure 15 in [1]). Further high statistics data for the \(\phi\) are needed to draw a solid conclusion on whether \(\phi\) follows number of constituent quark scaling or particle mass scaling and provide in detail the scaling behavior.

#### 3.3.5 Elliptic flow \(v_{2}\)

The measured \(\phi\) meson elliptic flow \(v_{2}\) as a function of \(p_{t}\) is shown by filled squares in figure 3.15. For comparison, \(v_{2}\) values for the \(K^{0}_{S}\) (filled triangles), \(\Lambda+\bar{\Lambda}\) (filled circles) and charged hadrons (open diamonds) measured by STAR [1, 1] are also shown. Lines represent \(\pi\), \(K\), \(p\) and \(\Lambda\)\(v_{2}\) calculated by hydrodynamical model [1]. The observed non zero \(v_{2}\) value indicates that the \(\phi\) has developed a significant amount of elliptic flow in \(Au+Au\) collisions. Since the \(\phi\) does not participate in the hadronic stage interactions, the non zero \(v_{2}\) must have been built up in a pre-hadronic stage. This is consistent with the scenarios of recombination/coaslescence models where \(v_{2}\) is assumed to have developed in partonic stage [2, 1, 2, 3]. The \(v_{2}\) scaled by number of constituent quarks as a function of \(p_{t}\) scaled by number of constituent is found to be consistent with one universal curve for several identified hadrons [1]. These results point directly to quark flow in the early partonic stages if this observation is further confirmed by more precise measurements.

The current measurements haven't been able to nail down whether the \(\phi\)\(v_{2}\) follows the mass hierarchy in the low \(p_{t}\) region (\(p_{t}<1.5\) GeV/c) as suggested by hydro models, and whether the \(\phi\) follows the number of constituent quark scaling in the intermediate \(p_{t}\) region (\(2.0\) GeV/c \(<p_{t}<5.0\) GeV/c) as indicated by recombination/coalescence models. Precise measurement of the \(\phi\)\(v_{2}\) as a function of \(p_{t}\) and collision centrality will shed more insight into the hadronization processes in heavy ion collisions and help disentangle different models.

