The origin of this peak has been traced back to the \(\Delta^{++}\to p+\pi^{+}\) and \(\Delta^{0}\to p+\pi^{-}\) decays, where the pions are misidentified as kaons. The reason is that there is inevitably some \(\pi^{\pm}\) contamination in the \(K^{\pm}\) sample when particles are identified by the measurement of \(<dE/dx>\) alone. Thus there should be some \(\Delta^{++}\) contaminations in the \(\Theta^{++}\) invariant mass distribution and some \(\Delta^{0}\) in the \(\Lambda(1520)\) invariant mass distribution. The \(\Delta^{++}\) and \(\Delta^{0}\) production rate is much higher, so a very small amount of contamination would result in a prominent peak in the \(\Theta^{++}\) and \(\Lambda(1520)\) invariant mass distribution.

Figure 4.4 shows the \(\Delta^{++}\) invariant mass distribution when the decay daughter \(\pi^{+}\) is identified as \(K^{+}\).

Figure 4.4: The simulated \(\Delta^{++}\) invariant mass distribution when the decay daughter \(\pi^{+}\) is identified as \(K^{+}\).

